{{Infobox legislative term|name=1919 Confederal Convention|image=Tierra y Libertad flag.svg|image_size=|alt=|caption=Flag of the Cantonalists|body=[[Confederal Congress]]|meeting_place=[[Cabildo of Jatiel]]|election=|government=|opposition=|country={{flag|Balisca}}|term=[[w:October 3|3 October]] {{W|1919}}|before=[[1909 Confederal Convention]]|after=[[1929 Confederal Convention]]|website=<!-- {{URL|example.com}} -->|chamber1=|chamber1_image=|chamber1_image_size=|chamber1_alt=|membership1=|control1={{W|Libertarian Socialism|Libertarian Socialists}} ([[Anarchism in Balisca|Anarchists]])|chamber1_leader1_type=Chairman of the Congress|chamber1_leader1=[[Ildefonsa Herrero]]|chamber1_leader2_type=Congress Secretary|chamber1_leader2=[[Velasquita Alunda]]|chamber1_leader3_type=|chamber1_leader3=|chamber1_leader4_type=|chamber1_leader4=|chamber2=|chamber2_image=|chamber2_image_size=|chamber2_alt=|membership2=|control2=|chamber2_leader1_type=|chamber2_leader1=|chamber2_leader2_type=|chamber2_leader2=|chamber2_leader3_type=|chamber2_leader3=|chamber2_leader4_type=|chamber2_leader4=|session1_start=|session1_end=}} The '''1919 Confederal Convention''' ({{Lang-bl|La Convención Confederales de 1919}}) was a [[Confederal Convention|congress of representatives]] of [[W:Soviet (council)|councils]] of workers, peasants and anarchist militias, held on 3 October 1919 in [[Jatiel]], [[Luzamontaña]]. The congress was attended by 1248 delegates (226 from [[Castejón]], 167 from [[Araucaria]], 364 from the [[Cerdanya|Cerdanyan Soviet Republic]], 191 from [[Hesperia]], 163 from [[Bayara]], 89 from [[Estuaria]] and 48 from [[Pindorama]]). More than 90% of the delegates were [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchists]], 16 [[Communist Party of Balisca|communists]] and 7 were left-wing social federalists.

The convention resulted in the abolition of the Baliscan republican system and the widespread implementation of [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] and more specifically [[W:libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] principles throughout various portions of society, including the {{W|free migration|abolition of internal borders}}, {{W|secularization}}, and {{W|democratic socialism}}. Much of the [[economy of Balisca]] was put under {{W|worker control}}, while {{W|social welfare}} was significantly expanded through the {{W|redistribution of wealth|redistribution of income and wealth}}. Worker committees gained control of their factories, [[W:agriculture|agrarian]] areas became collectivized and run as libertarian socialist {{W|agricultural commune}}s.

The mass empowerment and embrace of various groups in this bottom-up system has been described as a "{{W|people's commune}}" or an "[[W:anarchism|anarchist]] {{W|republic}}", one based on the theories of {{W|anarcho-communism}} and built on a network of [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]], {{W|factory committee}}s and [[W:Popular assembly|popular assemblies]]. The assemblies were used by the local citizens as a form of {{W|referendum}}s, where decisions were made through {{W|direct democracy}}, and became the basis for [[W:Civil law (common law)|civil law]]. The vast majority of Balisca's political decisions were made independently of {{W|Central Authority}}, through a system of {{W|libertarian municipalism|radical libertarian}} {{W|self-governance}} at the [[Communes of Balisca|communal]] and [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] levels. Networks of "[[W:Soviet democracy|free councils]]" acted as institutions of {{W|participatory democracy}}, where issues would be discussed and dealt with directly.

